**A spy.** Spies deceive enemy agents to stop their sabotage. Inside a contest where both sides expect deception, this can pass the test, as long as no one fakes a surrender or breaks a sworn truce, because those let enemies stop fighting later. A spy who lies to their own overseers fails the test: if every spy did, no one could check what spies do. A spy who spies on their own country's parties fails too. If spies did this, those who run them would come to decide who governs, and would become a secret government, or the close allies of one. So a spy needs more restraint, and more honesty with overseers, than an open role does. {# 2026-10-14 spy case cut to one quote (wassname: "it's meant to be a tiny role. just sun tzu is fine"). Removed quotes, verified, for future editors: Christopher Andrew, The Secret World (2018) l.1100 (one-party states' monitoring of 'ideological subversion'); Church Committee Final Report Book II (1976) ('Too many people have been spied upon...'); Sherman Kent (1949) ('objectivity and integrity of judgement'); Allen Dulles, The Craft of Intelligence (1963) ch. 16 (safeguards 'lie in the character and self-discipline'). slop/research/20261014_spy_*.md. #}Allen Dulles, who directed the CIA, put the safeguard in the officers' character:

